</div>
</p>

Q: Cache friendly method to multiply two matrices A: <p>The word you are looking for is <strong>thrashing</strong>. Searching for thrashing matrix multiplication in Google <a href="http://penguin.ewu.edu/%7Etrolfe/MatMult/MatOpt.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">yields more results</a>.</p>
<p>A standard multiplication algorithm for c = a*b would look like</p>
<pre><code>void multiply(double[,] a, double[,] b, double[,] c)
{
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)
            for (int k = 0; k &lt; n; k++)
                c[i, j] += a[i, k] * b[k, j]; 
}
</code></pre>
<p>Basically, navigating the memory fastly in large steps is detrimental to performance. The access pattern for <strong>k</strong> in B[<strong>k</strong>, j] is doing exactly that. So instead of jumping around in the memory, we may rearrange the operations such that the most inner loops operate only on the second access index of the matrices:</p>
<pre><code>void multiply(double[,] a, double[,] b, double[,] c)
{
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
    {
        double t = a[i, 0];
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)
            c[i, j] = t * b[0, j];

        for (int k = 1; k &lt; n; k++)
        {
            t = a[i, k];
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)
                c[i, j] += t * b[k, j];
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This was the example given on that page. However, another option is to copy the contents of B[*, j] into an array beforehand and use this array in the inner loop calculations. This approach is usually <strong>much faster</strong> than the alternatives, even if it involves copying data around. Even if this might seem counter-intuitive, please feel free to try for yourself.</p>
<pre><code>void multiply(double[,] a, double[,] b, double[,] c)
{
    double[] Bcolj = new double[n];
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)
    {
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; n; k++)
            Bcolj[k] = b[k, j];

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
        {
            double s = 0;
            for (int k = 0; k &lt; n; k++)
                s += a[i,k] * Bcolj[k];
            c[i, j] = s;
        }
   }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why isn&#39;t React considered MVC? A: <h2>React is not <em>considered</em> to be MVC</h2>
<p>React isn't <em>considered</em> MVC because it doesn't map very well with how MVC has been conceived and used on the back-end.</p>
<p>React is a rendering library and ideally just takes care of the View layer. It doesn't have a centralized orchestrator/router nor entirely separate (REST-style) controllers as MVC architectures on the back-end typically came to have.</p>
<h2>Since Facebook inaccurately contrasted Flux with MVC</h2>
<p>In delineating a shift with existing approaches, the Flux architecture was contrasted with MVC (read Faceboook's <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2014/05/facebook-mvc-flux/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MVC Does Not Scale, Use Flux Instead</a>).</p>
<p>But <strong><a href="https://legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2014/05/06/flux.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Flux</a> is not in direct opposition to MVC per se, but in opposition to mutability and two-way data-binding</strong>, something which MVC does not specify (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model%E2%80%93view%E2%80%93controller" rel="nofollow noreferrer">it even says</a> Views simply &quot;renders presentations of the model&quot;).</p>
<p>You could argue that the Flux architecture simply reframes MVC, renaming Model-View-Controller to Store-View-Dispatcher... Although a Dispatcher is somewhat <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2014/05/facebook-mvc-flux/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">more limited</a> than a Controller. Since a Dispatcher doesn't contain app logic to command models and views like a Controller would. But the Dispatcher just passes along commands defined elsewhere: opening up for the logic to be co-located with the View components.</p>
<p>The important difference is not the architectural entities per se, nor their names, but <em>how you restrain them</em>:</p>
<p><strong><a href="https://legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2014/05/06/flux.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Flux</a> disallows the two-way data-binding between Models and Views that people often allowed when using MVC</strong>. This restriction <a href="https://overreacted.io/the-bug-o-notation/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">makes debugging easier</a> (just trace up the React tree, instead of back-forth between components, and in the case of mutability: you'd have to trace also into each preceding component that used the value), and avoids performance issues like cascading state changes (bouncing back-forth between Models and Views, with subtle timing issues) that people experienced with two-way (aka. 2-way, bidirectional) data binding. (Even though <a href="https://imfeld.dev/writing/how_svelte_makes_two_way_binding_safe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">two-way data-binding actually </a><em><a href="https://imfeld.dev/writing/how_svelte_makes_two_way_binding_safe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">can</a></em><a href="https://imfeld.dev/writing/how_svelte_makes_two_way_binding_safe" rel="nofollow noreferrer"> be made safe</a>, as Svelte does.)</p>
<h2><em>Stateless MVC</em> also didn't fit on the client-side, and we wanted stateful clients</h2>
<p>The popular implementation of MVC on the <em>server</em> side had come to be synonumous with <em>statelessness</em>,  since web servers are generally stateless. It also interpreted <em>separation of concerns</em> in terms of technology layers (Models as the DB/ORM layer, Controllers as the routing layer, and Views as the HTML with embedded code layer - all working in unison to serve incoming requests in a stateless manner, between the client and the DB).</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/u46eY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/u46eY.png" alt="Stateless server-side MVC" /></a></p>
<p>Image credit to: <a href="https://acko.net/blog/model-view-catharsis/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://acko.net/blog/model-view-catharsis/</a></p>
<p>But as <a href="https://dev.to/ryansolid/comment/19pb3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Ryan Carniato from SolidJS</a> points out, on the evolution of <em>client</em> side MVC frameworks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>«Basically, the stateless MVC architecture was the wrong one for the client.«</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since rich clients (SPA's) typically need to be stateful. I.e. rich clients need to preserve user state, and quickly handle user interactions, without necessarily requiring a round-trip to the server.</p>
<h2>But React <em>is</em> MVC, in some sense</h2>
<p>That said, you could say that <strong>React actually is MVC in some sense</strong> (closer to the original meaning of MVC <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model%E2%80%93view%E2%80%93controller" rel="nofollow noreferrer">1</a>).</p>
<p>React is slicing the MVC vertically (by concern), instead of horizontally (by technology) which was what people had come to do with the MVC pattern. See, MVC had become a <a href="https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/software-architecture-patterns/9781491971437/ch01.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">layered architecture</a> with the Views as a thin layer on top. (Although not originally meant as such [1]).</p>
<p>Thus, you could say Components in React started out as small vertically-sliced encapsulated MVC's: containing both state/useState (model), rendering (view), and control-flow logic (a localised mini-controller, if you will).</p>
<p>Currently, Components (in front-end libraries like React and SolidJS) mix rendering with rendering logic. Components have come to range from entirely View-oriented, to entirely Control-oriented. This <a href="https://dev.to/redbar0n/what-happened-to-components-being-just-a-visual-thing-22hc" rel="nofollow noreferrer">can cause conceptual confusion</a>.</p>
<p>But most components are somewhere in between; having a bit of both rendering output and control-flow-logic.</p>
<h2>Components are ViewControllers, typically</h2>
<p>So currently, React Components have become more of a vertically sliced ViewController, where the Model bit is contained somewhere else. E.g. in state handling libraries like the centralised Redux, or in separate/orthogonal Recoil stores. Or merely pseudo-contained in the Component with hooks like <code>useState</code> (whereas in reality contained by React itself).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, <strong>Components can currently be seen as ViewControllers</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's interesting to see the similarity of React Components with iOS' UIViewControllers [2]. Maybe this mental model from native iOS development influenced the creation of Components, considering Facebook with the React + React Native combo intended to have the same framework and mental model for working with pure Native and the Web.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>'A View Controller (VC) manages views and helps in making the application’s UI. It coordinates with model objects and other controller objects. It is known for playing the role for both view objects and controller objects. Each VC displays its own views for the app content.' - <a href="https://www.codementor.io/@hemantkumar434/view-controller-lifecycle-ios-applications-7oyju9lp6" rel="nofollow noreferrer">iOS ViewController lifecycle</a></p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you need further convincing, just read <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/view_controllers" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apple's documentation of ViewControllers in Swift</a>, and imagine they are talking about a React Component. There is virtually no resistance (aka. cognitive dissonance).</p>
<h2>Components as Views, Hooks as Controllers</h2>
<p>These days, with a lot of component logic extracted into Hooks, <a href="https://twitter.com/ryanflorence/status/1056284116763602944" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ryanflorence</a> and <a href="https://twitter.com/Swizec/status/1406647598824361988" rel="nofollow noreferrer">swizecteller</a> have noted that you could see:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Components more as mere Views, and Hooks as Controllers</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the <a href="https://remix.run/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Remix</a> SSR framework for React, <a href="https://twitter.com/victorsavkin/status/1468668169816621056?s=20&amp;t=qU4FP4OdKXUTdzdoDVWxaw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">victorsavkin</a> has noted that:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>&quot;the <strong>loader+action combo</strong> is similar to the <strong>Controller</strong> in MVC. And the React component is basically the View.&quot;</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h2>A basic React MVC example</h2>
<p>This is a basic Todo-app example of how you may think of MVC in the context of React.</p>
<p>(It also exemplifies one very basic conceptualization of the &quot;functional core, imperative shell&quot; architecture, implemented with React.[3])</p>
<pre class="lang-jsx prettyprint-override"><code>function App() {


  // MODEL

  const [todos, setTodos] = useState&lt;Todo[]&gt;(() =&gt; {
    // Load todos from a data source (e.g., API, localStorage)
    return [
      { id: '1', title: 'Learn React', completed: false },
      { id: '2', title: 'Implement Onion Architecture', completed: true },
    ];
  });


  // CONTROLLER - Imperative Shell (I/O Operations)

  const handleAddTodo = (title: string) =&gt; {
    const newTodo = {
      id: crypto.randomUUID(),
      title,
      completed: false,
    };
    // ... Save the new todo to an external data source (e.g., API, localStorage) ...
    // Save it also using React's built-in state management.
    setTodos((prevTodos) =&gt; [...prevTodos, newTodo]);
  };

  const handleToggleTodo = (todo: Todo) =&gt; {
    const updatedTodo = {
      ...todo,
      completed: !todo.completed,
    };
    // ... Update the todo in the external data source ...
    // Update it also using React's built-in state management.
    setTodos((prevTodos) =&gt;
      prevTodos.map((t) =&gt; (t.id === todo.id ? updatedTodo : t))
    );
  };

  const handleDeleteTodo = (id: string) =&gt; {
    // ... Delete the todo from the external data source ...
    // Delete it also using React's built-in state management.
    setTodos((prevTodos) =&gt; prevTodos.filter((todo) =&gt; todo.id !== id));
  };


  // VIEW - Functional Core (React Components)

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;TodoForm onAddTodo={handleAddTodo} /&gt;
      &lt;TodoList
        todos={todos}
        onToggleTodo={handleToggleTodo}
        onDeleteTodo={handleDeleteTodo}
      /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}

// SUB-VIEWS

const TodoForm = ({
  onAddTodo,
}: {
  onAddTodo: (title: string) =&gt; void;
}) =&gt; {
  const [newTodoTitle, setNewTodoTitle] = useState('');

  const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent) =&gt; {
    e.preventDefault();
    onAddTodo(newTodoTitle);
    setNewTodoTitle('');
  };

  return (
    &lt;form onSubmit={handleSubmit}&gt;
      &lt;input
        type=&quot;text&quot;
        value={newTodoTitle}
        onChange={(e) =&gt; setNewTodoTitle(e.target.value)}
      /&gt;
      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Add Todo&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  );
};

const TodoList = ({
  todos,
  onToggleTodo,
  onDeleteTodo,
}: {
  todos: Todo[];
  onToggleTodo: (todo: Todo) =&gt; void;
  onDeleteTodo: (id: string) =&gt; void;
}) =&gt; {
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;ul&gt;
        {todos.map((todo) =&gt; (
          &lt;TodoItem
            key={todo.id}
            todo={todo}
            onToggle={onToggleTodo}
            onDelete={onDeleteTodo}
          /&gt;
        ))}
      &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
};

const TodoItem = ({
  todo,
  onToggle,
  onDelete,
}: {
  todo: Todo;
  onToggle: (todo: Todo) =&gt; void;
  onDelete: (id: string) =&gt; void;
}) =&gt; (
  &lt;li&gt;
    &lt;input
      type=&quot;checkbox&quot;
      checked={todo.completed}
      onChange={() =&gt; onToggle(todo)}
    /&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;{todo.title}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; onDelete(todo.id)}&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
);
</code></pre>
<p>Here we have the handlers (controller logic) at the top, in App. But you could imagine moving it down into the <code>TodoForm</code>, <code>TodoList</code>, and <code>TodoItem</code> components, so they go from being mere Views to more like ViewControllers.</p>
<p>In our case it makes sense to have the have the Model <code>Todo[]</code> at the App level, since it is the lowest common place where the handlers can be created and sent off into separate sub-trees (<code>TodoForm</code> and <code>TodoList</code>). You typically want to keep Model state for each individual component at the lowest possible place in the React tree.</p>
<h2>MVC Widgets</h2>
<p>So if a component manages it's own state (aka. <em>model</em>), in addition to it's handlers (aka. <em>controller</em>) and the rendered <em>view</em>, then you could call it a <em>componentized ModelViewController</em>, or better yet: an <strong>&quot;MVC Widget&quot;</strong>.</p>
<p>Which is what MVC was actually originally envisioned as, by Trygve Reenskaug. [1]</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/Ehmvl.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/Ehmvl.png" alt="MVC Widgets" /></a></p>
<p>Image credit to: <a href="https://acko.net/blog/model-view-catharsis/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://acko.net/blog/model-view-catharsis/</a></p>
<h3>ModelController + View, as a co-located MVC widget with separation of concerns</h3>
<p>If you want to see another conceptualization of MVC-widgets than the one above, where the Model state is <em>not</em> stored <em>within</em> the React components (but still <em>alongside</em> them), see <a href="https://gist.github.com/redbar0n/545e94cc4e0ca1dfc922459251fd7c5a" rel="nofollow noreferrer">What an </a><strong><a href="https://gist.github.com/redbar0n/545e94cc4e0ca1dfc922459251fd7c5a" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MVC-widget</a></strong><a href="https://gist.github.com/redbar0n/545e94cc4e0ca1dfc922459251fd7c5a" rel="nofollow noreferrer"> could look like with React+XState</a> Benefits of dividing up the MVC-widget into <strong>ModelController + Views</strong> in such an approach, is that the business logic inside the ModelControllers can be tested independently of rendering, and your React views can be cleaner. <a href="https://xstate.js.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">XState</a> also has the benefit of making all states explicit with names, and the state changes into explicit transitions, which can prevent bugs (e.g. impossible/faulty states). You can also use it to run <a href="https://stately.ai/docs/xstate-v4/xstate/model-based-testing/intro" rel="nofollow noreferrer">automatic model-based tests</a> of your app, and visualize the whole thing as a Statechart diagram.</p>
<h3>Notes</h3>
<p><strong>[1]</strong> -
It's interesting to note that front-end frameworks (React etc.) has more or less inadvertently (in their &quot;departure from MVC&quot;) actually implemented MVC more true to <a href="https://www.duo.uio.no/bitstream/handle/10852/9621/Reenskaug-MVC.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">how MVC was originally conceived by Trygve Reenskaug</a> back in 1979 (who already implemented it with Smalltalk). It was implemented as slicing up the interface/screen into a myriad of tiny atoms/components which each follow a MVC pattern: See <em>&quot;Every little widget on the screen had its own Model, Controller and View.&quot;</em> at <a href="https://youtu.be/WpkDN78P884?t=1826" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MVC is not an Architecture</a>. Although this approach has some drawbacks, especially related to syncing state changes, as noted at the section starting with <em>&quot;Sometimes MVC is applied at the individual widget level ...&quot;</em> in <a href="http://acko.net/blog/model-view-catharsis/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this excellent and visual MVC explainer article</a>.</p>
<p>In React however, syncing state was/is handled through 'prop drilling' (which becomes its own problem, solved by &lt;Context&gt; or centralized state libraries like Redux) combined with the one-way data flow of the Flux architecture (introduced by Facebook and often used with React, such as with Redux). See previous section.</p>
<p><strong>[2]</strong> -
It should be noted that the iOS ViewController - just as a Controller in an MVC pattern on the backend - might have more wide-reaching
concerns (like cross-component-communication, less encapsulation) than a controller in the sense that a React Component is a ViewController. Since React Components are a part of the JSX/DOM hierarchy and Flux architecture of React. There are probably a few different definitions about what a &quot;Controller&quot; actually means. But for my intents and purposes I treat it as a center for control-flow logic (be it used for simply view rendering, or, as in the backend case, also for routing and model orchestration).</p>
<p><strong>[3]</strong> -
&quot;<a href="https://www.destroyallsoftware.com/screencasts/catalog/functional-core-imperative-shell" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Functional Core, Imperative Shell</a>&quot; (aka. Onion/Hexagonal) architecture, explained and implemented in Ruby, by Gary Bernhardt. NB: Our React example does not have pure functions like you'd ideally want to have. Since the React view components call the (imperative) handlers in the outer shell, and thus produce side-effects.</p>

Q: httpclient and (decorating) middleware in golang A: <p><code>rts</code> is a slice of <code>Decorator</code>s. A <code>Decorator</code> is a <em>function</em> that takes in a <code>RoundTripper</code> and returns another <code>RoundTripper</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>for _, rt := range rts {
    decorated = rt(decorated)
}
</code></pre>
<p>^ that loops over decorators, <em>invoking</em> each one and passing the result to the next one.</p>
<p>Perhaps better variable names could make this code more readable</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>func Decorate(t http.RoundTripper, decorators ...Decorator) http.RoundTripper {
    decorated := t
    for _, decorate := range decorators {
        decorated = decorate(decorated)
    }
    return decorated
}
</code></pre>
